# local.zsh.example — template for machine-specific overrides.
# Copy this file to ~/.config/zsh/local.zsh (never commit that copy).
# local.zsh is git-ignored; it is sourced last by index.zsh.
#
# This file is for values that must WIN over the managed layers — aliases, tokens,
# editor choices. It is NOT the place to make a tool findable.
#
# PATH and FPATH belong in ~/.zshrc, ABOVE the managed block (ADR-0062). Being
# sourced last means every `command -v` guard has already run and compinit has
# already read fpath, so a PATH set here arrives too late: the tool's completions
# and integrations are skipped for that shell. The classic symptom is a completion
# that only starts working after `exec zsh` — the second shell inherits the
# exported PATH that this file set too late in the first one.
#
# Examples:

# Private PATH (work tools, internal binaries) — see the note above: put this in
# ~/.zshrc above the managed block if anything in the managed layer needs to find it.
# export PATH="$HOME/work/bin:$PATH"

# Machine-specific editor override:
# export EDITOR="code --wait"

# Private tokens (never committed):
# export MY_SERVICE_TOKEN="your-token-here"

# Weather alias (location-specific — keep here, not in committed aliases.zsh):
# alias meteo='curl -4 http://wttr.in/YOUR_CITY'
